{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"spring-clean-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Whole-home spring clean track","description":"Runs a yearly whole-home deep clean in gated steps - scope and schedule, declutter, living spaces top to bottom, kitchen and bathrooms, then floors and home checks - without redoing work.","category":"housekeeping","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","operate","maintain"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["plan","checklist","table"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["spring-clean","deep-clean","seasonal-cleaning","chemical-safety"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["plan-decluttering","deep-clean-kitchen","deep-clean-bathroom","plan-cleaning-schedule"],"personas":["cleaning-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"home","description":"The home room by room, floor types and special surfaces, the state it is in, and anything you want to focus on.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"time_and_people","description":"How much time you have and over what period (one long weekend, an hour each evening for two weeks), who can help, children, pets, allergies or asthma, and the climate or season.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"scope","file":"steps/01-scope.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"declutter","file":"steps/02-declutter.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"living-spaces","file":"steps/03-living-spaces.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"kitchen-and-bathrooms","file":"steps/04-kitchen-and-bathrooms.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"floors-and-checks","file":"steps/05-floors-and-checks.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs a whole-home deep clean one approved step at a time, in the order professionals use: plan, clear clutter before cleaning, work top to bottom and dry to wet, and do floors last so nothing is cleaned twice. Each step produces one plan or checklist and stops for approval; later steps build on approved versions. Every step uses one cleaning product at a time and never mixes products, respects surface limits, and keeps tasks within the time the household gave. Jobs that need a professional (heavy mould, chimney sweeping, gas appliance servicing, work at height outside) are flagged, not attempted. If the person asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"scope","file":"steps/01-scope.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Scope and schedule\n\nDecide what this spring clean covers and when.\n\nHome: {{home}}\nTime and people: {{time_and_people}}\n\n1. If the rooms, time available or who is helping are missing, ask for them in one message and stop.\n2. List the deep-clean tasks per room that go beyond the usual weekly clean (for example inside cupboards, behind and under furniture, windows and frames, curtains, mattresses, light fittings, vents, skirting boards), marking each as must-do or nice-to-have.\n3. Estimate rough time per task and fit the must-dos into the time available; move the rest to a later list. Show the totals.\n4. Write a dated schedule that follows the order of the remaining steps (declutter, living spaces, kitchen and bathrooms, floors and checks), with tasks assigned to helpers, including age-appropriate jobs for children.\n5. List supplies, starting with what they already have, and the safety basics: gloves, ventilation, one product at a time, products stored away from children and pets.\n\nStop for approval. Do not start cleaning instructions yet."},{"id":"declutter","file":"steps/02-declutter.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Declutter before cleaning\n\nClear what does not belong so the cleaning is faster.\n\n1. Give a quick declutter pass per room in the approved schedule: rubbish, things that belong in other rooms, and an obvious donate or sell pile, with a time limit per room.\n2. Set up four bags or boxes (bin, recycle, donate or sell, put away elsewhere) and a rule to take the donate pile out of the home within a week.\n3. Leave sentimental items and big decisions for another time; this pass is about clearing surfaces and floors.\n4. Note anything found that needs fixing or a professional (damp, pests, damaged cables) and add it to a separate list.\n\nStop for approval."},{"id":"living-spaces","file":"steps/03-living-spaces.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Living spaces, top to bottom\n\nClean bedrooms, living areas and halls in an order that avoids redoing work.\n\n1. For each room, give the order: ceilings, cobwebs and light fittings (switched off and cool, with a stable step, never standing on chairs), tops of wardrobes and doors, walls and switches, windows and frames inside, curtains and blinds (washed per care labels or vacuumed), upholstery and mattresses (vacuumed, aired, and spot-cleaned per their labels), furniture inside and out, skirting boards.\n2. Match methods to surfaces (painted walls tested in a hidden spot, wood dusted with a barely damp cloth, fabrics per care label).\n3. Keep floors for step 5, except moving furniture to clean behind it.\n4. Give a short check per room before moving on.\n\nStop for approval."},{"id":"kitchen-and-bathrooms","file":"steps/04-kitchen-and-bathrooms.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Kitchen and bathrooms\n\nDeep clean the wet rooms.\n\n1. Kitchen: start slow jobs first (soaking oven racks and extractor filters, defrosting the freezer), then cupboards inside one at a time with an expired-food check, appliances switched off and unplugged, oven per its manual, fridge with food kept cold, splashback and worktops, sink and drain, bins.\n2. Bathrooms: limescale and soap scum with a product suitable for each surface, grout, toilet, shower head, drains cleared mechanically first, extractor grille; mould inside silicone means replacing the sealant.\n3. Never mix products. Keep acid tasks (limescale) and any bleach task separate, rinse well and ventilate between them, and never add a second product to a toilet bowl that already contains one.\n4. Respect surface limits: no acids on natural stone, no abrasives on acrylic, stainless steel, glass hobs or gloss units.\n\nStop for approval."},{"id":"floors-and-checks","file":"steps/05-floors-and-checks.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Floors and home checks\n\nFinish with floors and the once-a-year checks.\n\n1. Floors last, top floor down and back of each room toward the door: vacuum including edges and under furniture, then mop hard floors with the method their surface takes (damp not wet for wood and laminate), and spot-clean or arrange professional cleaning for carpets and rugs as needed.\n2. Home checks to do while everything is moved: test smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, clean or change filters that the manuals say need it (extractor, vacuum, air purifier, tumble dryer lint housing), check under sinks for leaks, and note anything for a professional (gas appliance servicing, chimney sweep, damp or roof issues).\n3. Take the donate pile out and book the professional jobs from the list.\n4. End with a short weekly routine and a list of the nice-to-have tasks moved from step 1, spread over the coming months."}]}
